Build wordLengths

Read `line` from left to right. Here, a word is a group of characters with no spaces inside it. Record the length of each word. Ignore extra spaces before, after, or between words. For "a  cat", `wordLengths` is [1, 3].

Return the answer

Create a list `positiveValues` that keeps only the numbers in `wordLengths` that are greater than 0, in the same order. Complete `wordSlipsPositiveProofStrip` so it returns `positiveValues`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `wordLengths` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function wordSlipsPositiveProofStrip(line) {
  const a=line; let out=[];
  let length=0;for(let i=0;i<=a.length;i++){if(i===a.length||a[i]===' '){if(length)out.push(length);length=0;}else length++;}
  return out.filter(x=>x>0);
}


